To make this function more efficient for handling large amounts of text, we can use memory-mapped files (mmaps) instead of loading the entire text into memory at once. Here's an updated implementation:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1File, text2File)
    % Read text1 and text2 into memory-mapped files
    mmapOptions = 'r';
    mmapSize = max(32 * 1024 * 1024, stat(text1File).size); % Allocate at least 32 MB
    mmapText1 = mmread(text1File, mmapOptions, mmapSize);
    mmapText2 = mmread(text2File, mmapOptions, mmapSize);

    % Convert text to lowercase and split into words using mmapped memory
    mmappedWords1 = lower(strsplit(mmapText1));
    mmappedWords2 = lower(strsplit(mmapText2));

    % Find unique words using sets
    uniqueWords = setdiff(mmappedWords1, mmappedWords2);

    % Close memory-mapped files
    mmclose(mmapText1);
    mmclose(mmapText2);
end
```

In this implementation, we first set up the options for reading the files using `mmread()`. We also allocate at least 32 MB of memory for each file using `stat()` to ensure that there is enough space for the largest possible text size. Then, we convert the text to lowercase and split it into words using mmapped memory. Finally, we find the unique words using sets and close the memory-mapped files using `mmclose()`. This implementation should be much more efficient for handling very large text files than the original one, which loads all the text into memory at once.

