Here's an R function that takes a character vector as input and returns `TRUE` if all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric, otherwise it returns `FALSE`. The function uses the `tryCatch()` function to catch any conversion errors and returns `FALSE` if such errors occur.

```R
isNumericVector <- function(x) {
  is.numeric <- TRUE
  for (i in seq_along(x)) {
    if (is.na(x[i])) next
    tryCatch({
      x[i] <- as.numeric(x[i])
    }, error = function(e) {
      is.numeric <- FALSE
      return(FALSE)
    })
  }
  is.numeric
}
```

Let's test this function with some examples:

```R
# Test cases
x1 <- c("1", "2", "3", NA, "4")
x2 <- c("1", "a", "3", NA, "4")
x3 <- c(1, 2, 3, NA, 4)

isNumericVector(x1)
# [1] TRUE

isNumericVector(x2)
# [1] FALSE

isNumericVector(x3)
# [1] TRUE
```

In the first example, `x1`, all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric, so the function returns `TRUE`. In the second example, `x2`, not all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric, so the function returns `FALSE`. In the third example, `x3`, all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric, so the function returns `TRUE` again.

```typescript
import { Injectable, NestMiddleware } from 'nestjs';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';
import { map } from 'rxjs/operators';

@Injectable()
export class EnvironmentCheckMiddleware implements NestMiddleware {
  constructor() {}

  use(req: any, res: any, next: () => Observable<any>): void | Observable<any> {
    const env = process.env.NODE_ENV;

    // If in development mode, proceed to the next middleware
    if (env === 'development') {
      return next();
    }

    // Return false to prevent further processing if not in development mode
    return new Observable((observer) => observer.next(false));
  }
}
```

This middleware checks the `process.env.NODE_ENV` variable to determine whether the application is running in development mode or not. If it's in development mode, it allows the request to proceed to the next middleware by calling `next()`. If it's not in development mode, it returns an observable that immediately emits `false`, effectively stopping further execution of the middleware chain. You can apply this middleware globally using the `apply()` method provided by NestJS:

```typescript
// app.module.ts

import { MiddlewareConsumer, Module } from '@nestjs/common';
import { EnvironmentCheckMiddleware } from './middleware/environment-check.middleware';

@Module({})
export class AppModule {
  configure(consumer: MiddlewareConsumer) {
    consumer.apply(EnvironmentCheckMiddleware).forRoutes('*');
  }
}
```

With this setup, the middleware will be applied to all routes in your application, and only requests made during development mode will be allowed to continue through the middleware stack. Requests made outside of development mode will be stopped at this middleware and won't be passed on to any other middleware. Note that you may want to adjust the `forRoutes()` option based on your specific needs. For example, you could restrict the middleware to certain routes instead of applying it globally. I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any questions.

