Here's an implementation of the Naive Bayes algorithm using Accord.NET library for text classification:

First, let's define our `Category` enumeration to represent the three categories:

```csharp
enum Category { Sports, Politics, Entertainment }
```

Next, we'll create a class for holding labeled examples:

```csharp
class LabeledExample
{
    public string Text { get; set; }
    public Category Category { get; set; }
}
```

Now, let's load the labeled examples from a file:

```csharp
string[] lines = File.ReadAllLines("data.txt");
List<LabeledExample> examples = new List<LabeledExample>();
for (int I = 0; I < lines.Length; i++)
{
    string[] parts = lines[i].Split('\t');
    string text = parts[0];
    Category category = (Category)Enum.Parse(typeof(Category), parts[1]);
    examples.Add(new LabeledExample { Text = text, Category = category });
}
```

We'll convert our labeled examples into a matrix of word counts using the `CountVectorizer` class:

```csharp
CountVectorizer vectorizer = new CountVectorizer();
var matrices = vectorizer.FitTransform(examples.Select(e => e.Text));
```

We can now create a Naive Bayes classifier using the `MultinomialNB` estimator:

```csharp
MultinomialNB naiveBayes = new MultinomialNB();
naiveBayes.Fit(matrices);
```

To make predictions on new examples, we can pass them through the fitted classifier:

```csharp
string sentence = "LeBron James scored 45 points last night.";
double[] probabilities = naiveBayes.Transform(vectorizer.Transform(sentence));
Console.WriteLine($"Probability of Sports: {probabilities[0]:F2}, Probability of Politics: {probabilities[1]:F2}, Probability of Entertainment: {probabilities[2]:F2}");
